 # Execution policy
 
 `mgis::BatchExecutionPolicy` is shared by the behaviours, the models and
-the material properties:
-
-- `queue`: queue of the computations, whose type depends on the backend
-  (`cudaStream_t`, `hipStream_t`, pointer to a `sycl::queue`, to an
-  instance of a `Kokkos` execution space or to a resource of camp matching
-  the execution policy of `RAJA`). A null pointer denotes the default
-  queue, if meaningful for the backend (a queue is required by the `SYCL`
-  backend). With `RAJA`, it is the default stream of the current GPU with
-  `CUDA` and `HIP`, and the default resource of camp otherwise.
-- `preserve_stack_size`: if true, the stack size of the threads of the
-  device is never modified, and an error is reported if it is too small.
-  Otherwise, the stack size is increased if required. This affects the
-  whole process, since the stack size is a property of the context of the
-  device (`CUDA` and `HIP`).
+the material properties. Its member `backend_policy` points to the
+execution policy of the backend of the library, or is null for the
+default policy of the backend:
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+struct BatchExecutionPolicy {
+  const mfront::generic_parallel::AbstractBackendExecutionPolicy*
+      backend_policy = nullptr;
+};
+~~~~
+
+The execution policies of the backends are defined by `TFEL`
+(`MFront/GenericParallel/` headers, for instance
+`CUDABackendExecutionPolicy` or `SyclBackendExecutionPolicy`, see the
+documentation of the `generic-parallel` interface of `MFront`). They give
+the queue of the computations (`CUDA` or `HIP` stream, `SYCL` queue,
+instance of a `Kokkos` execution space, resource of camp for `RAJA`) and
+the options common to the backends (`preserve_stack_size` and
+`asynchronous`). `MGIS` only declares their base class
+(`MGIS/BackendExecutionPolicy.h`) and gives the pointer to the generated
+functions: it still does not depend on `TFEL` at compile time. The codes
+creating policies include the headers of `TFEL` and link the
+`MFrontGenericParallelRuntime` library of `TFEL`:
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+#include "MFront/GenericParallel/CUDABackendExecutionPolicy.hxx"
+const auto backend_policy =
+    mfront::generic_parallel::makeCUDABackendExecutionPolicy(
+        {.stream = stream});
+auto policy = BatchExecutionPolicy{};
+policy.backend_policy = &backend_policy;
+~~~~
+
+The library refuses a policy of another backend. The identifier of the
+policy it expects (for instance `cuda`, `sycl:oneapi` or `kokkos:Cuda`) is
+given by the `execution_policy` member of the description of the
+behaviour or of the material property, read by
+`LibrariesManager::getGenericParallelExecutionPolicy`. A null pointer
+denotes the default policy of the backend: the default stream with
+`CUDA` and `HIP`, the default instance of the execution space with
+`Kokkos`, and, with `RAJA`, the default stream of the current GPU with
+`CUDA` and `HIP` and the default resource of camp otherwise. The `SYCL`
+backend has no default policy: a policy holding a queue must be given. The
+generated functions copy what they need from the policy, which does not
+have to outlive the call.
+
+If the `preserve_stack_size` option of the backend policy is true, the
+stack size of the threads of the device is never modified, and an error
+is reported if it is too small. Otherwise, the stack size is increased if
+required. This affects the whole process, since the stack size is a
+property of the context of the device (`CUDA` and `HIP`).
 
 The overloads without execution policy use the policy of the material data
 manager, or a default policy for the rotations and the material
-properties. The default policy gives a null queue, which the `SYCL`
-backend refuses.
+properties. The default policy gives a null backend policy, which the
+`SYCL` backend refuses. The backend policy pointed to by the policy of a
+material data manager or of a material data view must stay valid as long
+as this policy is used.
 
 With this policy, the computations are synchronous: the functions return
-once the computations are completed. This is the default: the functions
-without execution policy are synchronous.
+once the computations are completed. A backend policy requesting
+asynchronous computations (`asynchronous` option) is refused, since such
+computations require statistics, which only the functions taking an
+asynchronous execution policy allocate. This is the default: the
+functions without execution policy are synchronous.
 
 ## Asynchronous computations
 
 Each batch function also exists with an asynchronous execution policy
-(`mgis::AsynchronousBatchExecutionPolicy`). The function then returns once
-the computations are submitted to the queue. The calling code can submit
-other computations, for instance the batches of other materials, and
-synchronize the queue once.
+(`mgis::AsynchronousBatchExecutionPolicy`). If its backend policy requests
+asynchronous computations (`asynchronous` option), the function returns
+once the computations are submitted to the queue. The calling code can
+submit other computations, for instance the batches of other materials,
+and synchronize the queue once. With the default backend policy, or a
+backend policy without the `asynchronous` option, the computations are
+synchronous, and the returned pending computation gives the result as for
+an asynchronous computation.
 
 ~~~~{.cxx}
+#include "MFront/GenericParallel/CUDABackendExecutionPolicy.hxx"
+const auto backend_policy =
+    mfront::generic_parallel::makeCUDABackendExecutionPolicy(
+        {.common_options = {.asynchronous = true}, .stream = stream});
 auto p = AsynchronousBatchExecutionPolicy{};
-p.queue = stream;
+p.backend_policy = &backend_policy;
 p.resource = &resource;  // managed memory
 p.location = MemoryLocation::SHARED;
 const auto pending = integrate(p, m, opts, dt);
